  const [startedAt] = useState(() => Date.now());
  ...
}
```

"The time since this page saw it start" — the component mounts when indicator shows; timer from mount. If the indicator re-mounts (e.g., toggling between local/remote recording?), elapsed resets. Also if `recorder` prop changes, the component stays mounted, elapsed keeps from original mount — fine-ish.

BUT: a real bug — `useElapsed` uses `useState(() => Date.now())` for startedAt and `useState(startedAt)` for now. The interval updates `now` every second, re-rendering the indicator. That's fine. But `title` attr on span includes recorder — fine.

Hmm wait, actually there's a subtle issue: the indicator might be mounted while `indicatorOn` false→true transitions; when the indicator unmounts and remounts (e.g., recorder changes identity), elapsed resets to 0 showing wrong duration. Minor.

29. **recording-notice.tsx** - Cases. `state.status === "local-recording" && state.pending.length > 0` — late-joiner note. OK.

`role="status" aria-live="off"` — hmm, `role="status"` has implicit aria-live="polite"; overriding with aria-live="off" — deliberate (announced by room status region). OK.

30. **use-recording-ui.ts** -

```js
function useNoticeAnnouncements(recording, nameOf, announce) {
  const { notice } = recording;
  const previous = useRef(notice);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (previous.current === notice) return;
    previous.current = notice;
    if (notice !== null) announce(noticeText(notice, nameOf));
  }, [notice, nameOf, announce]);
}
```

Bug: When a notice appears and is dismissed (notice → null), previous updates to null silently — fine. But consider two consecutive different notices: first notice A announced; dismissed; notice B appears → announced. OK. But what if notice A is replaced directly by notice B without going through null? previous !== notice → announce B. OK.

However: `notice: state.status === "idle" ? state.notice : null` in useRoomRecording — the notice is only surfaced when idle. If a new notice arrives while status is not idle (e.g., recording), it's masked (null), then when status returns to idle, notice appears → announced then. But if the same notice object persists across... fine.

`useIndicatorAnnouncements`: `previous.current = indicatorOn` initialized on first render; skip on first render. On transitions announce started/stopped. But note: "Recording stopped" announced whenever indicatorOn goes false — including when *this* participant's recording stopped with a notice that also gets announced ("The recording stopped.") → double announcement. Minor.

Also `announce(recordingHere ? startedHere : startedElsewhere)` — `recordingHere` captured at effect run; deps include recordingHere. Edge: indicatorOn turns on while recordingHere also true — announce "You're recording". OK.

`actionFor`: `if (state.status === "remote-recording")` blocked with reason "X is already recording." Hmm wait — during `remote-recording`, a late joiner gets prompted; while prompted, status would be "prompted"? For the non-recording participants not prompted, status "remote-recording" → Record control blocked. Fine.

Now let me look for the more serious issues:

**A. `useRecordingPlayback` per library card — opening every recording file.** In `recording-items.tsx`, every `RecordingCard`/`RecordingRow` calls `usePosterUrl` → `useRecordingPlayback(row.id)` → `recordings.library.openPlayback(id)` for each row. Dozens of recordings → dozens of OPFS file opens + object URLs + `<video>` elements with preload=metadata each fetching the blob. Performance/memory concern, and possibly hitting browser limits on concurrent OPFS access. But functionally it works. Severity: WARNING (performance/memory)? It also means the object URLs for all recordings stay alive while library is open. It's a design decision ("a frame of the recording itself"). I'd call it a SUGGESTION/WARNING. Since reviewers are told not to report style nits but real defects — memory blowup with large recordings? Object URLs from `getFile()` don't necessarily read the file into memory; they reference. The video elements with preload=metadata will read some data. For dozens of files this is heavy but bounded. I'll report as SUGGESTION maybe.

**B. Focus loss in local-recordings-screen on initial load with detail hash.** Actually let me re-check: On first render, `previousHash.current = hash` initialized, effect returns early — so no focus management on initial mount. Fine, no bug there. But the case I mentioned: hash change to detail while `list.loading`... after client nav from library, list already loaded. From NotFoundView? Minor.

Wait, actually there IS a focus bug: when the user navigates from library to detail, the effect focuses `main h1` — which at effect time is the detail's h1. But `focusPageHeading` sets `tabindex="-1"` attribute directly on the DOM node if missing — detail h1 already has tabIndex={-1}. OK. When returning to library via Back, `focusItem(item)` — the card link. The link exists once the list renders. The effect runs after the router re-renders. But careful: `list.recordings` — after returning, still loaded. OK.

But **the "back to library" navigation from NotFoundView or after delete when the row was the last**: `focusItem` fails → focusPageHeading → h1 "Recordings". OK.

**C. `useReloadOnOpen`** — `latest.current = reload;` assigned during render (not in effect) — this is a mutation during render, generally acceptable pattern for "latest ref" though strictly should be in effect/useLayoutEffect. With React 19 concurrent rendering, mutating refs during render is discouraged but common. Not reporting.

**D. `DeleteRecordingDialog` trigger** — `AlertDialogTrigger render={<Button variant="destructive" />}` renders the Delete button; the dialog is also controlled `open={confirmOpen}`. Clicking trigger opens; but the parent's `onOpenChange` guards `if (!remover.pending) setConfirmOpen(open)`. Fine.

**E. `RecordingDetailView` `onDeleted` focus flow** — After delete, `onDeleted(row)` is called; it sets `returnItem.current` to the *next* saved row id (by current sort) and navigates. Then hash effect focuses that item. But note `onDeleted` computes `saved` from `list.recordings` **which still includes the deleted row** (change event timing aside). `saved.findIndex(entry => entry.id === row.id)` → index; next = saved[index+1]. If the deleted row was last, `saved[index + 1]?.id ?? null` → null → focus h1. Matches spec ("the card that followed it, or the h1 when none did"). OK.

**F. register-recording-entities `subscribe` — `library.get` failure silently ignored** (`if (!active || !found.ok) return;`) — a transient failure drops the change. Minor.

**G. `RecordingPoster` seek logic**: `onSeeked={() => setFrame("shown")}` — fine. But `seekToFrame`: `video.currentTime = Math.min(1, duration/2)`. If duration is NaN (unknown, as with MediaRecorder WebM), uses 2 → min(1, 1) = 1s. Seeking may fail on such files; then `onSeeked` never fires, frame stays "waiting", video opacity-0 → shows film glyph. OK fallback.

**H. `useVideoPlayer` — `document.fullscreenEnabled` evaluated during render.** For SSR mismatch — this is a client app; fine.

**I. `recording-player.tsx` `keyAction`** — `case "ArrowDown": return onScrubber ? ... : null;` — ArrowUp/Down on scrubber seek ±5s; on range inputs, ArrowUp/Down natively change value... but the range is the scrubber itself; the handler intercepts and calls seekBy which sets currentTime and `setTime`, and the controlled `value` prop updates. OK.

However — **`event.preventDefault()` on Home/End etc.**: fine.

One notable a11y problem: `keyAction` intercepts **single-letter keys (m, f, k) everywhere in the region except SELECT** — including when focus is on the Download/Delete buttons outside? No — the handler is on the player `<section>`; buttons outside the player aren't affected. Inside the region: buttons play/mute/fullscreen — pressing "m"/"f"/"k" while a Button is focused triggers player actions and prevents default. Standard media-player shortcut pattern; acceptable.

**J. `storage-strip.tsx`** — `onKeep`: `requestPersistence().then(() => { setAnswer("asked"); ... })`. Then `persistLine`: if `persisted` true → "Protected..."; else if answer==="asked" → "The browser didn't allow this." — **Bug**: If the user clicks "Keep recordings" and the browser **grants** persistence, `info.persisted` updates only if the entity query refetches. The mutation `normalize`s the returned row into the graph, so `query.items[0]` should update via the mutation's normalization. The mutate returns `{id, ...await requestPersistence()}` — presumably returns `{persisted: true, usedBytes, quotaBytes}`. So the query cache updates. OK.

But — if `requestPersistence()` **rejects** (e.g.,NotAllowedError), `.then` never runs; `answer` stays "none"; button reappears (requesting false); no feedback, focus stays on button; user confused. Unhandled promise rejection. Minor error-handling gap: `void requestPersistence().then(...)` without catch → unhandled rejection console noise and no UI feedback. WARNING-ish/SUGGESTION.

**K. `useRecordingStorage` — `query.items[0]`** — The storage entity `list` returns rows `[await readStorage()]`. If `library.storage()` throws when unsupported? In LibraryView, StorageStrip is rendered only when support !== "unsupported". OK.

**L. `useDeleteRecording` error typing**: `error: mutation.state.error` typed `string | null`. The generic third param `LocalRecording` as the error type is suspicious. If the library's `useEntityMutation<Id, Success, Error>` — passing `LocalRecording` as Error type means `mutation.state.error` is `LocalRecording | null`, which is not assignable to `string | null` → compile error. OR the third generic is something else (e.g., variables/context). Without the library signature, can't be certain. Hmm. In `use-recording-storage.ts`: `useEntityMutation<void, RecordingStorageRow, RecordingStorageRow>` with `normalize: (row) => ...`. So third param might be the "normalized output" type. Then in use-delete-recording, third param `LocalRecording` would be the normalized type but there's no normalize and mutate returns `true` (not LocalRecording). Mismatch? `mutate: async (id) => { ... return true; }` — returns boolean, second generic is `true`. Third `LocalRecording`... unclear. I'll not report this since I can't verify library semantics.

**M. `with-room-recording.ts` leave ordering** — `await recording.end()` before `inner.leave()` — dispose stops recording and "tells the others while still connected". OK.

But `join`: `recording.begin()` is called after `inner.join` resolves OK. If `begin` is called while a previous consent session exists (rapid leave/join?), detach disposes previous asynchronously (`void previous.dispose()`), and the previous recorder's stop could race with the new session. Edge case, minor.

**N. `compose-recordings.ts` createRecorder replaces recorder without disposing the old one** — Documented "Call it once per page". If the app calls it per join, previous recorder may still be recording; the library's `localActiveId` now points to the new recorder, and the old recorder's index writes... potential leak but per contract.

**O. RecordingIndicator `useElapsed` timer with `max-bp481:hidden`** — fine.

**P. `recording-notice.tsx` — the `requesting` note replaces the one-time notice** — fine.

**Q. Accessibility: `library-view.tsx` Toolbar rendered *after* `StorageStrip`; `section aria-labelledby` wrapping. Fine.

**R. `RecordingRow` nested interactive elements?** The row is an `<li>` with a Link and a download anchor — separate elements, fine.

**S. `RecordingCard` — the whole card is a Link containing spans (no nested interactive) — fine. `title={recordingTitle(row)}` for truncation. OK.

**T. `local-recordings-screen.tsx` — `data-testid={list.loading ? undefined : "km-recordings-library"}`** — fine.

